<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Merged into the app bundle's Info.plist by tauri-build (and embedded
  into the dev binary via embed-plist, so `tauri dev` gets it too).

  WKWebView routes getUserMedia through the host app's TCC permissions:
  without NSCameraUsageDescription macOS refuses the capture sandbox
  extension ("Could not create a 'com.apple.webkit.camera' sandbox
  extension") and the camera silently yields nothing — the exact
  black-preview failure observed on the Mac. Microphone is declared for
  the same reason (voice capture runs natively through cpal today, but
  any WebView-side getUserMedia({audio}) call needs it).

  NSCameraUseContinuityCameraDeviceType: Apple requires this opt-in for
  AVCaptureDeviceDiscoverySession — and therefore WebKit's camera device
  manager — to surface iPhone Continuity Cameras (developer.apple.com
  "Supporting Continuity Camera in your macOS app"). Relevant on Macs
  with no built-in camera where an iPhone may be the only device.

  Note these declarations make capture possible only in the bundled
  .app — WebKit's GPU helper resolves camera access against the host's
  bundle identity, so `tauri dev`'s bare binary cannot capture video
  regardless of TCC grants (tauri-apps/tauri#11951). See
  docs/contributor/development.md.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Rekindle uses your camera for video calls in voice channels and direct calls.</string>
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Rekindle uses your microphone for voice chat in voice channels and direct calls.</string>
	<key>NSCameraUseContinuityCameraDeviceType</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
